  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Saturday/...\nOn Tuesday, the Pacific low will slowly weaken as it moves \nnortheast into NV/UT before moving into the northern Plains on \nWednesday. At the same time, a weak ridge over SE TX will \nstrengthen slightly resulting in a return of southwesterly flow \nover the Desert Southwest. This will result in a gradual return of\nmoisture across southern Arizona. This increase in moisture and \nsome upper level forcing from the aforementioned low, slight \nchances for showers and storms will return to the higher terrain \nareas in northern and eastern Arizona. After this low advects into\nthe northern Plains by the middle of the week, weak large scale \ntroughing will remain across the eastern Pacific/West Coast \nthrough the end of the week. By the end of the week, the high \npressure system over TX will try to retrograde over NM/AZ, but it \nwill be battling the large scale troughing over the West. \nEnsembles show PWATs staying around 1.0-1.3\" across southern AZ \nthrough the end of the week. As a result, NBM/WPC PoPs for the end\nof the week continue to show 30-50% across the higher terrain of \nsouthern Gila County and 10-20% across the south- central AZ lower\ndeserts.\n\n\nTemperatures this coming week will remain largely unchanged, but are \nexpected to dip a couple degrees midweek as the low pressure system \nmoves to the north of our area and cause H5 heights to dip to around \n582-585 dm. Regardless, afternoon highs will generally be in the \nupper 90s to low 100s across the lower deserts, and in the upper 80s \nto mid 90s across the higher terrain. Morning lows will generally be \nin the 70s to to low 80s across the lower deserts, and in the 60s \nacross the higher terrain areas. Morning lows should start to creep \nup by the end of the upcoming workweek (more morning lows in the low \n80s across the lower deserts). As a result, widespread minor \nHeatRisk will continue through the first half of the week with \nmoderate HeatRisk returning to the lower deserts for the latter half \nof next week as the morning lows creep up and daily normals \ndecrease.",
